{"context":"The two planes in the architecture have opposite requirements. Control-plane traffic — presence, text, call setup, queue drain, key distribution — must be reliable, ordered and schema-evolvable across client versions that will not upgrade simultaneously. Data-plane audio must be timely, and reliability actively harms it: a retransmitted frame that arrives after its playback deadline is worthless, and waiting for it delays every frame behind it. A single transport cannot serve both well.","decision":"**Control plane** — one WebSocket per client to the coordinator, carrying protobuf-encoded envelopes with a `oneof` payload per message type. The schema is versioned and unknown fields are ignored, so a newer coordinator and an older client remain interoperable.\n\n**Data plane, real-time voice (stage 2)** — UDP with RTP framing, reusing an established RTP implementation for sequence numbers, timestamps, loss signalling and jitter buffering rather than inventing an audio wire format. Explicitly not TCP: head-of-line blocking converts one lost packet into a stall across every packet behind it.\n\n**Data plane, file transfer (stage 2)** — a TCP stream, manifest first, then 1 MiB content-addressed chunks each carrying its own hash. TCP is correct here because the payload has no deadline and completeness is the whole point.\n\n**Message identity** — a client-generated ULID per message, plus a coordinator-assigned monotonic per-recipient position used for queue resumption. The ULID makes redelivery idempotent; the position makes resumption cheap.","consequences":"**Positive.** Protobuf keeps the control plane compact on constrained links and gives a real schema-evolution story for a fleet that upgrades unevenly. Reusing RTP avoids reimplementing a jitter buffer, which is a well-known source of subtle audio defects. Content-addressed file chunks make resume trivial and make re-sending a similar file nearly free.\n\n**Negative.** A code-generation step enters the build, so the schema becomes a build dependency and a review surface. Three framings — protobuf, RTP, chunked stream — must each be tested. And ULIDs are larger than sequential integers, a cost accepted because they can be generated offline without coordination, which is exactly what the offline outbox needs.","alternativesConsidered":"**JSON over WebSocket.** No code generation, trivially debuggable by eye, and fast to build. Rejected on two counts: bandwidth on the constrained links named in the vision, and the absence of any schema discipline for a fleet that will run mixed client versions.\n\n**One TCP connection for everything, including audio.** Considerably simpler — one transport, one reconnect path, no UDP. Rejected because it makes the stage-2 latency target unreachable in principle rather than merely difficult.\n\n**CBOR or MessagePack.** Compact and schema-light. Viable, but rejected because the schema-evolution guarantees are weaker exactly where this system needs them, at version skew across a fleet.\n\n**A bespoke audio framing instead of RTP.** Rejected: it would re-solve sequence numbering, timestamping and loss signalling, and would almost certainly solve them worse than an implementation that has been exercised in production."}